@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+import type { Finding } from "../finding.page-type.ts"
+
+export const thePipelineSopsStoreIsSixCopiesAndOneValueNoPageHolds = {
+  id: "01a076cb-024e-721d-9bdf-598474d631c0",
+  pageTypeSlug: "finding",
+  slug: "the-pipeline-sops-store-is-six-copies-and-one-value-no-page-holds",
+  domainSlug: "domain/secret",
+  claim:
+    "`pipeline-secrets.sops.yaml` was decrypted and compared against every secret page, which is what an earlier finding said would settle it. Six of its seven values are held by a page already. The seventh, `GOTRUE_DB_PASSWORD`, is held by no page and is not inside the gotrue database url that would carry it, so taking the file away loses that one value and only that one. Two of the six copies are stale rather than current: the `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` here is not the cnpg superuser's password, and it is byte for byte the `AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD` beside it.",
+  evidence:
+    "Read 2026-09-06. Every value below was compared by sha256 prefix and byte length; no value was printed, and each was piped from sops straight into the comparison.\n\nWHAT THE STORE HOLDS, against the 76 secret pages that hold a value.\n`AGE_SECRET_KEY` 481449cfef0f, 74 bytes — `pipeline-engine-secrets-age-secret-key`.\n`ALANWALTON_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` 08212c434157, 586 bytes — `pipeline-engine-secrets-supabase-service-role-key`.\n`SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` 08212c434157, 586 bytes — the same page, so those two keys were already one value.\n`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` 88ab57a730f0, 53 bytes — `cloudflare-api-token`.\n`AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD` 7a827513cb5c, 64 bytes — `collections-secrets-postgres-password`.\n`POSTGRES_PASSWORD` 7a827513cb5c, 64 bytes — the same page again, so those two keys are one value too.\n`GOTRUE_DB_PASSWORD` 50d41d77c90e, 64 bytes — no page.\n\nWHY THE POSTGRES ONE READS AS STALE. The cnpg superuser's password is 1f97b8235620 at 40 bytes, read from `postgres-cnpg-superuser-password` while migrating that resource. The store's `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` is a different value of a different length, so it is not the superuser's password whatever it once was. One 64-byte value under both `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` and `AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD` is either two roles sharing one password or one key left behind by a rotation of the other.\n\nWHY THE GOTRUE ONE IS NOT A COPY. `gotrue-secrets-database-url` holds c3b98962db61 at 160 bytes, and `value.includes(password)` over it is false. A control on the same call asked whether that value contains its own first eight bytes, which answered true, so the false is a real absence rather than a comparison that cannot see. `gotrue-secrets-gotrue-jwt-keys` answers false the same way.\n\nSIGHTEDNESS. The census hashes every page's value into one map and looks each store value up. A made-up string was looked up on the same call and came back held by no page, so the map answers absence rather than answering nothing.\n\nSTILL NO READER. The earlier finding's grep remains true: nothing decrypts this file. Both manifests it named as neighbours have since been taken away, and their values are pages now.\n\nWHAT IS OWED. Whether `GOTRUE_DB_PASSWORD` is a live credential is not something the tree can answer, and taking the file away is not undoable. Either it becomes a page of its own and the file goes, or it is confirmed dead and the file goes.",
+} as const satisfies Finding
